.resources/BUG-260917-3lddu0/BUG-260917-3lddu0_rework-rev2.md:8:2. **P1-1 — the §2.4 property has no owner independent of the append gate, and the registry binds one anyway.** The brief required two properties with two sets of tests and said not to let one stand in for the other. Your candidate changes no production line outside `internal/sessrepo`; `sessprofile.Derive` is byte-identical to trunk and takes `(record, events)` only, so it cannot see the lease store and a losing event that extends its own lease's tail is a perfectly continuous chain to it. With the gate disabled as an instrument, probe 15 still yields `{Profile: yolo, HasSource: true}` at `Projector.Project`. `TestLosingLeaseProfileEventIgnored` fails at its `AppendEvent(...) != ErrStaleLease` assertion and never reaches its `Derive`/`Run` assertions — its kill is the append gate's kill. The shipped harness never runs it at all, so task AC row 2 has no mutant. And `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json` binds acceptance case `story-260917-losing-lease-profile-source` with production owner `internal/sessprofile/profile.go:Derive` to §2.4 clause line 666, while the behaviour the test observes is produced by `sessrepo.checkWinningLease`. tracecheck cannot see a semantic misbinding — it pins declarations — so this one is on you.
.resources/BUG-260917-3lddu0/BUG-260917-3lddu0_rework-rev3.md:6:1. **P2-1 — the §2.4 clause binding you documented does not exist as an edge.** README says `story-260917-losing-lease-profile-source` adds its proof to clause `2.4#2`. The registry defines that acceptance case with the correct `AppendEvent` production owner, but the case occurs in **zero ownership-group or clause acceptance-case lists** — clause `2.4#2` still lists only `sessprofile-derive`. The reviewer decoded the registry independently and recorded one definition and zero references (`logs/registry-binding-census.json`; the case is around line 2880 and the clause around line 4464, README around line 3596). Renaming the test makes tracecheck fail, which proves only the global declaration check — it does not create the absent clause edge. So: connect the append-gate case to the section and clause it actually discharges, keep route (b)'s scope explicit in the case wording, **re-derive `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256` after the edit**, re-run tracecheck and the section-scoped runs, and align README with what the tool prints. Do not claim derivation-side authority you do not have, and do not close the unknown-higher-epoch ambiguity in prose.
